00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:03.790
And I think this is
one of the biggest

00:08:03.790 --> 00:08:06.700
value when you are working
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:08.470
You get Google as your SRE.

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:11.380
SRE stands for Sire
Reliability engineers.

00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:13.540
Today, when we manage
our production systems,

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:17.620
we have dedicated teams that
are responsible for managing

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:21.700
those production application for
Google and for our customers.

00:08:21.700 --> 00:08:25.690
And this is not about monitoring
and recovering your system,

00:08:25.690 --> 00:08:28.624
its about continuously
improving the production,

00:08:28.624 --> 00:08:30.790
creating more tools, more
monitoring solutions, more

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:34.000
automation to make sure that
everything is working better

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:35.230
all the time.

00:08:35.230 --> 00:08:37.720
And when you are a Google
Container Engine user,

00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:39.919
you benefit from that.

00:08:39.919 --> 00:08:42.909
So going back to the
Niantic story, when

00:08:42.909 --> 00:08:49.010
they saw the traffic is much
better than what they expected,

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:51.200
we just get the team together.

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:55.210
We had people from support,
from Niantic of course,

00:08:55.210 --> 00:08:59.570
SRE and engineering team working
together and making sure that

00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:03.620
players can continue and
play and find Pokemon's.

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:06.890
So what do you get when you
start a cluster on Google

00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:08.510
Container Engine?

00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:11.090
The first thing we'll do--
so it takes about every three

00:09:11.090 --> 00:09:15.290
minutes, and we will
provision your cluster,

00:09:15.290 --> 00:09:18.380
and you will get a control
plan, which we will

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:20.810
manage for you on your behalf.

00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:24.560
And from a user perspective, you
get a black box API endpoint,

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:27.790
and we take control
of all of that.

00:09:27.790 --> 00:09:30.910
But the main benefit is
not about the provisioning

00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:34.400
of the cluster, but the way that
we manage everything for you.

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:36.820
So we make sure the
control plan is up to date

00:09:36.820 --> 00:09:39.760
and we upgrade to the
latest Kubernetes version.

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:42.520
We monitor it, we make
sure it's available,

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:47.070
we make sure we have backups,
we make backups are working.

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:49.780
That's something that
sometimes we forget to do.

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:55.960
And we also provision your
VMs, but when we do that,

00:09:55.960 --> 00:10:00.340
we'd not just take the
standard Kubernetes version,

00:10:00.340 --> 00:10:02.800
but we also add some
add-ons that are required

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:04.600
to run production workloads.

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:07.970
If for example, you need an L7
load balancer or [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:07.970 --> 00:10:10.700
for monitoring on
your dashboard,

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:12.760
we make sure that
everything is available

00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:15.490
and you can immediately start
deploying your application,

00:10:15.490 --> 00:10:20.046
instead of worrying for
cluster configuration.

00:10:20.046 --> 00:10:21.670
I'm also happy to
announce that we just

00:10:21.670 --> 00:10:24.550
expanded our
responsibility, and now we

00:10:24.550 --> 00:10:27.190
are not just responsible
for your control plane,

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:30.910
we also do VM auto
repair and auto upgrade.

00:10:30.910 --> 00:10:32.530
This means that we
update Kubernetes

00:10:32.530 --> 00:10:36.370
to the latest version, we
monitor automatic repair.

00:10:36.370 --> 00:10:39.250
It's now available on beta
and I encourage you all

00:10:39.250 --> 00:10:42.040
to opt in to those features.

00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:44.740
This means that you get
all the innovation coming

00:10:44.740 --> 00:10:50.080
from Kubernetes, which if its
new features or security fixes,

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:53.387
all in a reliable manner.

00:10:53.387 --> 00:10:54.970
Now, let me hand
over to Robert and he

00:10:54.970 --> 00:10:57.630
will talk about auto scaling.

00:10:57.630 --> 00:10:58.800
ROBERT BAILEY: Thanks then.

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:01.380
So one common problem that
application developers face

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:04.050
is how to handle
traffic fluctuation.

00:11:04.050 --> 00:11:06.780
Some applications have a sort
of predictable diurnal cycle

00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:09.060
of traffic, while
other applications,

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:11.569
such as the one by
Groupby, have large spikes

00:11:11.569 --> 00:11:13.110
during holiday
seasons when you might

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:15.240
see a lot of people
purchasing on Black Friday

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:17.169
or leading up to Christmas.

00:11:17.169 --> 00:11:18.960
As an application
developer, you should not

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:21.940
need to worry about constantly
tuning your application.

00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:24.150
Google Container Engine
can take care of that

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:26.640
and scale the number
of VMs in your cluster

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:29.152
and the number of pods that
are serving your application.

00:11:29.152 --> 00:11:30.360
So first a bit of background.

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:31.500
I just mentioned pods.

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:32.850
What is a pod?

00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:35.100
Some of you are probably
very familiar Kubernetes,

00:11:35.100 --> 00:11:37.950
for others this may be
something new for you.

00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:40.624
A pod is a small
group of containers.

00:11:40.624 --> 00:11:42.540
It's not a one to one
mapping with containers,

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:44.581
it's one or more containers,
and this is actually

00:11:44.581 --> 00:11:46.810
a very important
distinction because there

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:49.830
are a lot of times when you want
to build an application where

00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:52.360
two things need to work
very closely together.

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:54.840
A pod is also the atomic
unit of scheduling

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:56.762
when you want to
schedule your application

00:11:56.762 --> 00:11:58.470
to run in your cluster,
it gets scheduled

00:11:58.470 --> 00:11:59.880
with pods being co-located.

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:01.110
Pods share an IP address.

00:12:01.110 --> 00:12:03.000
They share local disk.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:05.490
They share local IPC.

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:07.560
And so, a good
example of this is

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:09.690
if you wanted to launch
a simple web server.

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:11.790
You might have a
custom application that

00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:14.520
knows how to pull your
website content from GitHub,

00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:18.420
or from a Cloud Storage bucket,
or from a local on-prem version

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:20.400
control system,
and you pair that

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:22.110
with an off the
shelf generic web

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:24.210
server like Apache or NGINX.

00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:26.160
By putting these two
containers together,

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:28.440
you are then able to deploy
them as a single unit

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:31.020
where the data puller
can write the local disk

00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:33.450
and the web server can
serve off the local disk.

00:12:33.450 --> 00:12:35.550
The great thing about
this is that it decouples

00:12:35.550 --> 00:12:37.260
the application
development lifecycle

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:38.739
for the two pieces of code.

00:12:38.739 --> 00:12:40.530
You can push new versions
of the web server

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:41.850
if there's a security
fix, without having

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:43.650
to rebuild your
application, and vice versa.

00:12:43.650 --> 00:12:45.566
You can update your
application without having

00:12:45.566 --> 00:12:48.719
to rebuild the web server.

00:12:48.719 --> 00:12:50.760
So now imagine you're
running your small websites

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:53.130
and it starts to get popular.

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:55.530
So you could, quite
easily with Kubernetes,

00:12:55.530 --> 00:12:57.955
scale the number of replicas,
the number of copies

00:12:57.955 --> 00:12:59.330
of your website
that are running.

00:12:59.330 --> 00:13:02.580
You could start out with
one, go to two, go to four,

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:04.122
and easily handle
the traffic load.

00:13:04.122 --> 00:13:06.330
But you probably don't want
to spend all of your time

00:13:06.330 --> 00:13:08.820
constantly changing the number
of replicas of your websites

00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:09.360
that are running.

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:10.484
That sounds kind of boring.

00:13:10.484 --> 00:13:12.510
You want to spend time
building new websites.

00:13:12.510 --> 00:13:14.884
So what you can do is you can
turn on horizontal pod auto

00:13:14.884 --> 00:13:17.400
scaling, which will monitor
the incoming requests

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:19.290
load and the utilization
of your pods,

00:13:19.290 --> 00:13:22.710
and automatically scale
the number of pods for you.

00:13:22.710 --> 00:13:25.350
It will actually also scale down
if the traffic to your website

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:26.620
goes down as well.

00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:30.352
So you're not actually
using extra resources.

00:13:30.352 --> 00:13:31.060
So this is great.

00:13:31.060 --> 00:13:32.685
So the number of pods
keeps increasing,

00:13:32.685 --> 00:13:35.030
but now let's say your
website gets really popular

00:13:35.030 --> 00:13:37.471
and the number of VMs that
you have in your cluster

00:13:37.471 --> 00:13:39.720
is no longer sufficient to
run all of the replicas you

00:13:39.720 --> 00:13:41.460
need for your site.

00:13:41.460 --> 00:13:43.710
You can also enable a
cluster auto scaler,

00:13:43.710 --> 00:13:49.140
and a cluster auto scaler will
look at the unschedulable pods

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:50.910
that you've requested--
you or automation

00:13:50.910 --> 00:13:52.720
has requested to
run in your cluster,

00:13:52.720 --> 00:13:56.677
and will provision new VMs for
you to allow those pods to run.

00:13:56.677 --> 00:13:58.260
And it's actually
smart enough, if you

00:13:58.260 --> 00:14:00.450
have a cluster with
different types

00:14:00.450 --> 00:14:02.850
and shapes of VMs to
provision the right type of VM

00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:04.740
for your pod to
run, because it runs

00:14:04.740 --> 00:14:07.110
a simulation of if I
add a new machine type,

00:14:07.110 --> 00:14:08.490
will the pod actually run?

00:14:08.490 --> 00:14:09.990
And it doesn't add
VMs needlessly.

00:14:09.990 --> 00:14:13.950
It won't actually help
you scale your cluster up.

00:14:13.950 --> 00:14:15.780
Like horizontal
pod auto scaling,

00:14:15.780 --> 00:14:18.180
it will scale your cluster
down when the extra VMs

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:19.480
are no longer needed.

00:14:19.480 --> 00:14:22.200
So if you have a big traffic
spike, say for the holidays,

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:24.570
and then traffic goes back
to sort of a normal level

00:14:24.570 --> 00:14:26.540
after the holiday
event is over, you

00:14:26.540 --> 00:14:29.940
don't have to go and deprovision
VMs to save yourself money.

00:14:29.940 --> 00:14:33.300
The cluster auto scaler will
do that for you automatically.

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:35.880
One of the great things about
working on both Dual Container

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:39.081
Engine and Kubernetes is
that we often find cases

00:14:39.081 --> 00:14:41.580
where we can solve a problem
for our Google Container Engine

00:14:41.580 --> 00:14:44.237
users, and also for
the community at large.

00:14:44.237 --> 00:14:46.320
The cluster auto scaler
is a great example of this

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:48.528
because we recognized that
this was a problem that we

00:14:48.528 --> 00:14:51.840
had for our paying Google
Container Engine users,

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:53.340
but it was also a
problem for anyone

00:14:53.340 --> 00:14:54.857
who wanted to run Kubernetes.

00:14:54.857 --> 00:14:56.940
So in this case, what we
did is instead of writing

00:14:56.940 --> 00:14:59.100
a proprietary cluster auto
scaler that would only

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:00.930
work with Google
Container Engine,

00:15:00.930 --> 00:15:03.090
we actually wrote the
cluster auto scalar as a pod

00:15:03.090 --> 00:15:05.790
that you can run in any
Kubernetes deployment.

00:15:05.790 --> 00:15:07.680
So it runs just
like any other pod

00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:10.350
and it talks to the
computer's API to figure out

00:15:10.350 --> 00:15:12.240
when it should scale up.

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:15.740
And our implementation then uses
GCEs API to actually scale up

00:15:15.740 --> 00:15:18.000
their cluster, but the
core logic is reusable

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:19.950
and you can plug it
into any other system

00:15:19.950 --> 00:15:21.810
to scale a cluster
elsewhere, and we

00:15:21.810 --> 00:15:25.830
think that this is a great model
for how we can enable people

00:15:25.830 --> 00:15:27.780
to run on Google
Container engine,

00:15:27.780 --> 00:15:29.340
but feel like they
have the ability

00:15:29.340 --> 00:15:32.962
to run their cluster
anywhere else as well.

00:15:32.962 --> 00:15:34.420
So running Kubernetes
in production

00:15:34.420 --> 00:15:36.400
often means you have
multiple teams that

00:15:36.400 --> 00:15:39.110
needs different levels of
access to your cluster.

00:15:39.110 --> 00:15:41.379
It also means running
development testing staging

00:15:41.379 --> 00:15:43.420
environments, so you can
validate your co-changes

00:15:43.420 --> 00:15:44.985
before you push
them to production.

00:15:44.985 --> 00:15:46.360
In a Google
Container Engine, you

00:15:46.360 --> 00:15:49.517
can levers of power of Google
IEM and Kubernetes namespaces

00:15:49.517 --> 00:15:51.850
to make sure that the right
people have the right access

00:15:51.850 --> 00:15:53.510
to your cluster.

00:15:53.510 --> 00:15:55.870
So Kubernetes namespaces
divide your cluster up

00:15:55.870 --> 00:15:58.390
into logical
sub-domains, and each

00:15:58.390 --> 00:16:01.450
of these logical sub-domains
has different levels of access

00:16:01.450 --> 00:16:04.180
control, has its
own DNS hierarchy,

00:16:04.180 --> 00:16:06.880
so you can easily
segregate services,

00:16:06.880 --> 00:16:09.410
and you can use namespaces
for whatever you'd like.

00:16:09.410 --> 00:16:12.130
You can create a namespace for
every user in your department.

00:16:12.130 --> 00:16:13.450
You can create a
namespace for every app

00:16:13.450 --> 00:16:15.160
that you deploy and
you can use namespaces

00:16:15.160 --> 00:16:16.050
for different environments.

00:16:16.050 --> 00:16:18.091
So you can have a testing
namespace and a staging

00:16:18.091 --> 00:16:18.970
namespace.

00:16:18.970 --> 00:16:20.530
They're quite
flexible and it allows

00:16:20.530 --> 00:16:22.155
you to easily segregate
different parts

00:16:22.155 --> 00:16:23.770
of your application.

00:16:23.770 --> 00:16:26.615
One thing that we do recommend
is using a separate cluster

00:16:26.615 --> 00:16:28.990
for your production instances
because although namespaces

00:16:28.990 --> 00:16:31.720
are a great logical boundary,
they are not a very firm

00:16:31.720 --> 00:16:34.390
boundary in terms of
isolation, either for security

00:16:34.390 --> 00:16:39.360
or for interpod utilization.

00:16:39.360 --> 00:16:41.609
And with Kubernetes
1.6 coming out soon,

00:16:41.609 --> 00:16:43.150
you can use roll
base access controls

00:16:43.150 --> 00:16:46.240
to find IEM roles to permission
inside of a namespace, which

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:48.640
allows you to create very
fine grained controls of who

00:16:48.640 --> 00:16:51.310
can access resources
within different namespaces

00:16:51.310 --> 00:16:53.545
in a single cluster.

00:16:53.545 --> 00:16:56.170
So at Google, we're really proud
of the load balancing products

00:16:56.170 --> 00:16:58.360
that we offer as part
of the cloud platform.

00:16:58.360 --> 00:17:01.270
Our load balancing is built
on the same front end serving

00:17:01.270 --> 00:17:03.910
infrastructure that powers
services with billions

00:17:03.910 --> 00:17:06.339
of users like Google
Search, Google Maps, Gmail,

00:17:06.339 --> 00:17:07.300
and YouTube.

00:17:07.300 --> 00:17:09.369
With Google Container
Engine, your application

00:17:09.369 --> 00:17:11.740
can also take advantage of
these amazing capabilities,

00:17:11.740 --> 00:17:15.609
just like Niantic did when
they were building Pokemon GO.

00:17:15.609 --> 00:17:18.579
Google Container Engine offers
two levels of load balancing.

00:17:18.579 --> 00:17:20.544
The first is level
four load balancing.

00:17:20.544 --> 00:17:22.210
Level four load
balancing is appropriate

00:17:22.210 --> 00:17:24.099
if you need to terminate
TCP connections

00:17:24.099 --> 00:17:27.069
or if you need to
handle raw UDP packets.

00:17:27.069 --> 00:17:29.050
Level four load balancing
sends your traffic

00:17:29.050 --> 00:17:30.650
to what we call a service.

00:17:30.650 --> 00:17:32.620
A service in
Kubernetes is a group

00:17:32.620 --> 00:17:37.022
of pods that provide a stable
IP, and one thing you'll

00:17:37.022 --> 00:17:38.980
know if you want to run
containers that can get

00:17:38.980 --> 00:17:41.140
rescheduled, is that
containers can move around

00:17:41.140 --> 00:17:42.530
inside your cluster.

00:17:42.530 --> 00:17:43.990
And so by using a
service, you know

00:17:43.990 --> 00:17:45.948
that your traffic will
always get to your pods,

00:17:45.948 --> 00:17:50.130
even if those pods had been
relocated onto new VMs.

00:17:50.130 --> 00:17:53.079
External services are then
exposed with an IP address.

00:17:53.079 --> 00:17:55.120
By default, services are
internal and not exposed

00:17:55.120 --> 00:17:57.460
to the internet, but it's
quite easy to expose them.

00:17:57.460 --> 00:17:58.960
And on Google
Container Engine, this

00:17:58.960 --> 00:18:02.290
is done with the cloud
network load balancer.

00:18:02.290 --> 00:18:05.262
The other type of service that
we use is called an L7 service.

00:18:05.262 --> 00:18:06.970
So many applications
that are built today

00:18:06.970 --> 00:18:08.890
are built on top of
the web foundational

00:18:08.890 --> 00:18:11.854
standards of HTTP and HTTPS.

00:18:11.854 --> 00:18:13.270
We have an objective
in Kubernetes

00:18:13.270 --> 00:18:14.769
called an ingress
object, that makes

00:18:14.769 --> 00:18:18.250
it very easy for you to
configure this L7 load

00:18:18.250 --> 00:18:19.089
balancer.

00:18:19.089 --> 00:18:20.630
And the power of
the L7 load balancer

00:18:20.630 --> 00:18:23.530
is that with a single exposed
IP address and port or domain

00:18:23.530 --> 00:18:26.410
name, you can easily map
traffic to multiple services

00:18:26.410 --> 00:18:27.800
in your cluster.

00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:31.212
So you could map some traffic
to a static content service that

00:18:31.212 --> 00:18:33.670
is surveying static web pages
that have been pre-generated,

00:18:33.670 --> 00:18:36.370
and you can map other
parts of your URL paths

00:18:36.370 --> 00:18:39.792
to dynamic content if you
need to talk to a database.

00:18:39.792 --> 00:18:42.250
And all this can be expressed
within an object that is then

00:18:42.250 --> 00:18:44.740
declaratively applied
to your cluster,

00:18:44.740 --> 00:18:47.631
and then instantiated in terms
of a Google Cloud L7 load

00:18:47.631 --> 00:18:48.130
balancer.

00:18:53.170 --> 00:18:55.170
And with that, we're going
to switch over and do

00:18:55.170 --> 00:18:55.770
a quick demo.

00:18:59.520 --> 00:19:00.900
Excellent.

00:19:00.900 --> 00:19:03.470
So for this demo, I've
pre-provisioned two clusters

00:19:03.470 --> 00:19:04.580
in two different regions.

00:19:04.580 --> 00:19:07.710
So there's one cluster that's
in the us-west1 region and one

00:19:07.710 --> 00:19:10.215
cluster that's in the
europe-west1 region.

00:19:10.215 --> 00:19:12.090
And what I'm going to
show you is how easy it

00:19:12.090 --> 00:19:15.540
is to deploy an application
onto Google Container Engine,

00:19:15.540 --> 00:19:17.700
and get it up and running,
and serving real traffic

00:19:17.700 --> 00:19:19.720
to end users.

00:19:19.720 --> 00:19:21.630
So the first thing
that we are going to do

00:19:21.630 --> 00:19:25.080
is this application is going
to use a Cloud Spanner, which

00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:28.130
is a newly announced part
of Google Compute Engine.

00:19:28.130 --> 00:19:31.140
And Cloud Spanner
is a global database

00:19:31.140 --> 00:19:33.540
that has strong support
for transactions.

00:19:33.540 --> 00:19:35.670
It turns out, to talk
to Cloud Spanner,

00:19:35.670 --> 00:19:38.490
the identity model requires
you to use a service account.

00:19:38.490 --> 00:19:39.240
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:19:39.240 --> 00:19:41.781
going to create something called
a secret in Kubernetes, that

00:19:41.781 --> 00:19:44.160
encapsulates the client side
credentials of that service

00:19:44.160 --> 00:19:46.475
account to allow us
access to Cloud Spanner.

00:19:46.475 --> 00:19:48.850
Now, I haven't mentioned
secrets yet, but what secrets do

00:19:48.850 --> 00:19:52.500
is that secrets de-couple
the sensitive information

00:19:52.500 --> 00:19:55.680
of your application, such as
passwords or private keys,

00:19:55.680 --> 00:19:58.419
from the actual configuration
of your application.

00:19:58.419 --> 00:20:00.210
And this is great
because the configuration

00:20:00.210 --> 00:20:02.640
of an application, you generally
want to put in source control,

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:04.350
especially if you're
running a production

00:20:04.350 --> 00:20:06.090
because that makes it
easier for you to version

00:20:06.090 --> 00:20:07.680
and to share among
multiple people.

00:20:07.680 --> 00:20:09.540
But you really don't want
to check your passwords

00:20:09.540 --> 00:20:10.380
into source control.

00:20:10.380 --> 00:20:12.270
I know we've probably
all seen people

00:20:12.270 --> 00:20:14.686
will do a search of GitHub and
find all sorts of passwords

00:20:14.686 --> 00:20:16.960
on GitHub, which is not
really a best practice.

00:20:16.960 --> 00:20:19.812
So secrets allow you to separate
those two pieces of data.

00:20:19.812 --> 00:20:21.270
So the next thing
we're going to do

00:20:21.270 --> 00:20:23.085
is we're going to
create a deployment.

00:20:23.085 --> 00:20:25.380
A deployment is a
higher level controller

00:20:25.380 --> 00:20:26.864
that creates pods for you.

00:20:26.864 --> 00:20:28.530
Generally, you don't
want to create pods

00:20:28.530 --> 00:20:32.100
by yourself because a pod
has a limited lifetime.

00:20:32.100 --> 00:20:34.377
If a pod dies, it
will not be recreated,

00:20:34.377 --> 00:20:36.960
but if you create a higher level
controller like a deployment,

00:20:36.960 --> 00:20:39.043
the deployment is then
responsible for making sure

00:20:39.043 --> 00:20:41.050
that the pod is always running.

00:20:41.050 --> 00:20:43.909
So in this case, we've
created a deployment

00:20:43.909 --> 00:20:45.450
with a single replica,
which is going

00:20:45.450 --> 00:20:47.820
to launch a single pod for us.

00:20:47.820 --> 00:20:49.470
And in the deployment
specification

00:20:49.470 --> 00:20:50.928
there are lots of
different fields.

00:20:50.928 --> 00:20:53.040
Most of them are not
actually very important.

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:53.880
This one is pretty important.

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:56.296
This tells us which container
we're actually going to run.

00:20:56.296 --> 00:20:58.490
This deployment has
a single container.

00:20:58.490 --> 00:21:00.450
And then this container
is parametrised

00:21:00.450 --> 00:21:02.040
by a few environment variables.

00:21:02.040 --> 00:21:03.540
So through the
environment, we're

00:21:03.540 --> 00:21:04.950
going to tell it
where to connect to,

00:21:04.950 --> 00:21:06.949
like which Spanner database
instance since we're

00:21:06.949 --> 00:21:08.070
going to connect to.

00:21:08.070 --> 00:21:09.570
And then we're also
going to tell it

00:21:09.570 --> 00:21:12.340
where to get the credentials
to connect to that database.

00:21:12.340 --> 00:21:14.070
So we just created a secret.

00:21:14.070 --> 00:21:16.110
That secret is
automatically mounted

00:21:16.110 --> 00:21:20.396
by Kubernetes inside of
the pod at this location,

00:21:20.396 --> 00:21:22.770
and then the code will read
the secret from that location

00:21:22.770 --> 00:21:24.015
and connect to Spanner.

00:21:30.950 --> 00:21:31.955
Caps lock.

00:21:31.955 --> 00:21:33.350
There we go.

00:21:33.350 --> 00:21:37.420
So, if we then look at
our pods in the cluster,

00:21:37.420 --> 00:21:38.960
we'll see that we
have a single pod

00:21:38.960 --> 00:21:40.910
and it's now in
the running state.

00:21:40.910 --> 00:21:43.140
So while we were
inspecting the ML file,

00:21:43.140 --> 00:21:45.910
the scheduler took that
deployment, turned it

00:21:45.910 --> 00:21:48.020
into pods, found a
place to run the pod,

00:21:48.020 --> 00:21:50.020
and the system then fetched
that container image

00:21:50.020 --> 00:21:52.540
from Google Container
Registry, instantiated it,

00:21:52.540 --> 00:21:53.840
and now it's running.

00:21:53.840 --> 00:21:54.850
So that's great.

00:21:54.850 --> 00:21:58.260
So next what we're into is
we're going to create a service.

00:21:58.260 --> 00:22:00.600
So in this case, the
service that we're creating

00:22:00.600 --> 00:22:04.414
is a service with an
external load balancer.

00:22:04.414 --> 00:22:07.080
What this external load balancer
will do, as I explained before,

00:22:07.080 --> 00:22:09.570
is it will actually talk
to the Google Cloud APIs

00:22:09.570 --> 00:22:12.180
and create a load balancer
for you that will expose

00:22:12.180 --> 00:22:14.940
your service to the internet.

00:22:14.940 --> 00:22:16.440
If you leave this
last line off, you

00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:18.330
will create a service that
is only accessible inside

00:22:18.330 --> 00:22:18.996
of your cluster.

00:22:23.600 --> 00:22:26.572
If we list services, you can
see that the Kubernetes service

00:22:26.572 --> 00:22:27.530
is the default service.

00:22:27.530 --> 00:22:29.529
It's provided with your
cluster, and the service

00:22:29.529 --> 00:22:31.360
we just created its called Next.

00:22:31.360 --> 00:22:35.024
The external IP takes about
a minute or so to show up,

00:22:35.024 --> 00:22:36.440
and once that IP
shows up we'll be

00:22:36.440 --> 00:22:39.680
able to go ahead and look
at that service in the web

00:22:39.680 --> 00:22:44.410
browser, and see, and
load our web page.

00:22:44.410 --> 00:22:47.530
So while we're
waiting for that, we

00:22:47.530 --> 00:22:51.430
will go back and look at our
networking tab in the cloud

00:22:51.430 --> 00:22:52.480
console.

00:22:52.480 --> 00:22:55.120
And if we look at load
balancing, we should see,

00:22:55.120 --> 00:22:55.960
here's our service.

00:22:55.960 --> 00:22:58.340
So our service is now being
provisioned by the system.

00:22:58.340 --> 00:22:59.910
This is done
automatically for you.

00:22:59.910 --> 00:23:02.140
You tell Kubernetes I'd
like a service externalized,

00:23:02.140 --> 00:23:06.180
it goes ahead and
programs that for you.

00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:07.090
And there it goes.

00:23:07.090 --> 00:23:09.487
So now we have an
external IP address.

00:23:09.487 --> 00:23:10.820
So we'll go ahead and load that.

00:23:10.820 --> 00:23:11.580
Aha, there we go.

00:23:11.580 --> 00:23:12.134
Great.

00:23:12.134 --> 00:23:13.800
So our pod is serving
a simple web page.

00:23:13.800 --> 00:23:15.480
It tells us the name of the pod.

00:23:15.480 --> 00:23:21.040
So we can actually go
back and verify gt1t5.

00:23:21.040 --> 00:23:21.540
Great.

00:23:21.540 --> 00:23:23.956
Looks like it's matched up,
and it's telling us the region

00:23:23.956 --> 00:23:25.139
that it's running in.

00:23:25.139 --> 00:23:26.430
Now, our plot is pretty simple.

00:23:26.430 --> 00:23:30.240
It serves this data, but it also
writes an event into Spanner.

00:23:30.240 --> 00:23:32.010
And so if we go into
the cloud console,

00:23:32.010 --> 00:23:35.220
we can actually load up Spanner
and we can find the database

00:23:35.220 --> 00:23:37.380
that we provisioned,
and we can actually

00:23:37.380 --> 00:23:38.910
search in the database.

00:23:38.910 --> 00:23:41.070
And say OK, look,
we've got requests.

00:23:41.070 --> 00:23:43.650
They're all coming
in to us-west1.

00:23:43.650 --> 00:23:44.580
OK, that's great.

00:23:44.580 --> 00:23:46.200
So we've got our service.

00:23:46.200 --> 00:23:48.030
It's been pushed
into one cluster.

00:23:48.030 --> 00:23:49.031
It's externally exposed.

00:23:49.031 --> 00:23:51.071
If you guys want, you can
hit the same IP address

00:23:51.071 --> 00:23:52.770
and you'll get the
same response back.

00:23:52.770 --> 00:23:55.750
Each of those responses is
now being written to Spanner.

00:23:55.750 --> 00:23:58.170
So of the most powerful
things about Kubernetes

00:23:58.170 --> 00:24:00.350
is the way we've deployed
this application.

00:24:00.350 --> 00:24:02.580
It's very easy, but it's
also very easy to do it

00:24:02.580 --> 00:24:04.000
again, and again, and again.

00:24:04.000 --> 00:24:06.420
And so in this example,
we have two clusters

00:24:06.420 --> 00:24:08.260
in two different
regions of the world.

00:24:08.260 --> 00:24:10.500
And we can apply
exactly the same yaml

00:24:10.500 --> 00:24:14.130
files to our second cluster and
get exactly the same results.

00:24:14.130 --> 00:24:16.770
We can get the
same code running,

00:24:16.770 --> 00:24:18.180
and since it's
been parametrised,

00:24:18.180 --> 00:24:21.899
it will now tell us that
it's running in Europe.

00:24:21.899 --> 00:24:23.440
So to do this, what
we're going to do

00:24:23.440 --> 00:24:25.160
is we're going to
switch contexts.

00:24:25.160 --> 00:24:28.390
So the Kubernetes client
command line interface

00:24:28.390 --> 00:24:30.850
knows how to talk
to a single cluster,

00:24:30.850 --> 00:24:33.130
but it has credentials to
talk to multiple clusters.

00:24:33.130 --> 00:24:34.480
And using contexts,
you can actually

00:24:34.480 --> 00:24:35.854
switch between
different clusters

00:24:35.854 --> 00:24:37.422
that are all being configured.

00:24:37.422 --> 00:24:39.880
You can pass it onto command
line for each command you run,

00:24:39.880 --> 00:24:42.170
or in this case, we're going
to set the global context

00:24:42.170 --> 00:24:44.780
so we don't have to pass it
for each of the commands.

00:24:44.780 --> 00:24:46.900
The three commands
I'm copying now

00:24:46.900 --> 00:24:49.573
are exactly the three commands
that were previously run.

00:24:49.573 --> 00:24:51.947
They're just running them now
in a different environment.

00:24:59.092 --> 00:25:00.550
So again, if we
get deployments, we

00:25:00.550 --> 00:25:04.125
see that there's the same
name of the deployment.

00:25:04.125 --> 00:25:06.000
If we actually list the
nodes in our cluster,

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:08.090
we can now see that we are
indeed talking to the Europe

00:25:08.090 --> 00:25:09.589
cluster, and we're
no longer talking

00:25:09.589 --> 00:25:12.400
to the us-west1 cluster.

00:25:12.400 --> 00:25:13.900
And if we try to
get services again,

00:25:13.900 --> 00:25:15.700
we'll see that it's pending.

00:25:15.700 --> 00:25:17.830
If we run a query here again--

00:25:17.830 --> 00:25:19.450
so all of our access--

00:25:19.450 --> 00:25:21.020
it looks like we had a couple
that went to europe-west1.

00:25:21.020 --> 00:25:22.645
That's probably from
when I was testing

00:25:22.645 --> 00:25:24.160
prior to the presentation.

00:25:24.160 --> 00:25:25.838
I forgot to clear
out the database.

00:25:28.530 --> 00:25:29.670
That's still pending.

00:25:29.670 --> 00:25:31.625
So we'll give that just
a second to come up.

00:25:31.625 --> 00:25:33.000
We can check on
our load balancer

00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:35.420
again and watch it
get created, here.

00:25:35.420 --> 00:25:37.809
So on this page, we've got
the single load balancer that

00:25:37.809 --> 00:25:40.100
was there before, and shortly
we will get a second load

00:25:40.100 --> 00:25:41.214
balancer.

00:25:41.214 --> 00:25:43.762
OK, there it's coming up.

00:25:43.762 --> 00:25:46.095
So quite shortly here we
should have another IP address.

00:25:55.420 --> 00:25:56.420
And there it is.

00:25:56.420 --> 00:25:59.095
Excellent.

00:25:59.095 --> 00:26:00.470
And when we load
this IP address,

00:26:00.470 --> 00:26:02.570
instead of saying
us-west1, it should tell us

00:26:02.570 --> 00:26:04.040
now we're running in Europe.

00:26:04.040 --> 00:26:06.165
And again, it's telling us
the name of the pod that

00:26:06.165 --> 00:26:07.345
is hosting this application.

00:26:10.080 --> 00:26:13.440
So we can, if we want to
scale our replicas out,

00:26:13.440 --> 00:26:15.000
and we can see the
pod name change.

00:26:33.500 --> 00:26:35.090
So now we see that
we've requested

00:26:35.090 --> 00:26:38.077
there to be two replicas,
and if we reload the page,

00:26:38.077 --> 00:26:39.660
we should see it
flip between the two.

00:26:39.660 --> 00:26:43.150
It may take a second
for that to show up.

00:26:43.150 --> 00:26:44.800
So now we have our
service and it's

00:26:44.800 --> 00:26:47.630
been deployed into two different
regions, which is great.

00:26:47.630 --> 00:26:49.470
We can serve European
customers from Europe.

00:26:49.470 --> 00:26:51.700
We can serve US customers
from the US if we'd like.

00:26:51.700 --> 00:26:53.680
By pointing them to two
different IP addresses,

00:26:53.680 --> 00:26:55.450
we can map to domain names.

00:26:55.450 --> 00:26:58.090
But most people want to
have sort of a single entry

00:26:58.090 --> 00:26:59.980
point for all of
their customers,

00:26:59.980 --> 00:27:02.615
and one easy way to do that
is you can create a DNS entry,

00:27:02.615 --> 00:27:05.590
and you could put both of these
IP addresses in the DNS entry.

00:27:05.590 --> 00:27:08.170
That will cause traffic to
round robin between the two

00:27:08.170 --> 00:27:10.320
clusters, which is
perfectly reasonable,

00:27:10.320 --> 00:27:12.070
but we think a much
better solution

00:27:12.070 --> 00:27:15.935
is to use Google's Global
Cloud L7 Load Balancer.

00:27:15.935 --> 00:27:18.760
The L7 load balancer
will automatically

00:27:18.760 --> 00:27:21.490
push traffic to the two
clusters based on where

00:27:21.490 --> 00:27:23.960
the request is coming from.

00:27:23.960 --> 00:27:26.470
So for this demo, I've
pre-provisioned an L7 load

00:27:26.470 --> 00:27:27.365
balancer.

00:27:27.365 --> 00:27:29.740
You can see it's configured
to point to the two clusters,

00:27:29.740 --> 00:27:32.640
one in europe-west1,
and one in us-west1,

00:27:32.640 --> 00:27:35.680
and you can actually see that in
both cases, all of the backends

00:27:35.680 --> 00:27:36.670
are healthy.

00:27:36.670 --> 00:27:38.822
So the backends here
are the VMs, the nodes

00:27:38.822 --> 00:27:40.030
that are part of the cluster.

00:27:43.567 --> 00:27:45.150
And then if we go
look at the traffic,

00:27:45.150 --> 00:27:47.630
you can see that it turns
out we're sending traffic

00:27:47.630 --> 00:27:49.220
to both of these backends.

00:27:49.220 --> 00:27:51.080
So before the
presentation started,

00:27:51.080 --> 00:27:53.030
I started a while loop--

00:27:53.030 --> 00:27:55.160
I should do that more slowly--

00:27:55.160 --> 00:27:56.030
with a curl command.

00:27:56.030 --> 00:28:00.200
So I set up a DNS entry that
points to my L7 IP address,

00:28:00.200 --> 00:28:04.410
and just 10 times a second I'm
curling that from my laptop.

00:28:04.410 --> 00:28:07.220
And my laptop's on the
West Coast of the US,

00:28:07.220 --> 00:28:09.140
and so the majority
of the responses

00:28:09.140 --> 00:28:12.230
here are going to
be us-west1, if you

00:28:12.230 --> 00:28:14.810
watch this line as it toggles.

00:28:14.810 --> 00:28:16.670
But I set the L7
load balancer so

00:28:16.670 --> 00:28:19.970
that after getting five
requests per second to the US,

00:28:19.970 --> 00:28:22.640
it'll actually spill
over traffic to Europe.

00:28:22.640 --> 00:28:25.790
So what this means is that if
too many requests are coming

00:28:25.790 --> 00:28:28.440
in, they will overload
my set of backends,

00:28:28.440 --> 00:28:30.149
they'll spill over to
the second cluster.

00:28:30.149 --> 00:28:31.814
And you can see from
the terminal output

00:28:31.814 --> 00:28:33.290
here, that every
once in a while we

00:28:33.290 --> 00:28:36.830
are getting responses that
are spilling over to Europe.

00:28:36.830 --> 00:28:41.510
Likewise, if we load
the L7 IP address here,

00:28:41.510 --> 00:28:43.370
for the most part,
we will get us-west,

00:28:43.370 --> 00:28:45.269
but every once in a
while we'll get Europe

00:28:45.269 --> 00:28:46.810
because the traffic
is spilling over.

00:28:51.522 --> 00:28:52.230
So this is great.

00:28:52.230 --> 00:28:53.420
So now we've deployed
our application.

00:28:53.420 --> 00:28:55.003
We've deployed it
in multiple regions.

00:28:55.003 --> 00:28:57.140
We're serving traffic globally.

00:28:57.140 --> 00:28:58.830
What happens if
something goes wrong?

00:28:58.830 --> 00:29:01.280
So in this case,
we've got requests

00:29:01.280 --> 00:29:05.849
coming in from
both places and so

00:29:05.849 --> 00:29:07.890
we're going to switch back
to the us-west cluster

00:29:07.890 --> 00:29:15.200
by changing our
context, and we're

00:29:15.200 --> 00:29:17.270
going to delete our service.

00:29:17.270 --> 00:29:18.740
So this is simulating
what happens

00:29:18.740 --> 00:29:19.500
if something goes wrong.

00:29:19.500 --> 00:29:20.930
Now your service
no longer exists.

00:29:20.930 --> 00:29:24.140
Your service is no longer
exposed to the internet.

00:29:24.140 --> 00:29:26.540
If you go to the
first IP address,

00:29:26.540 --> 00:29:30.740
it was trying to serve us-west1,
it's not going to work anymore.

00:29:30.740 --> 00:29:32.790
Right, so that service
IP address is gone.

00:29:32.790 --> 00:29:35.840
So if you'd set up DNS, you'd
have two IP addresses that

00:29:35.840 --> 00:29:38.810
were being round robined,
and about half the time,

00:29:38.810 --> 00:29:41.470
your customers would
stop getting responses.

00:29:41.470 --> 00:29:43.880
And even worse, with
DNS propagation delays,

00:29:43.880 --> 00:29:45.620
if you want to
change the DNS entry,

00:29:45.620 --> 00:29:49.270
it's going to take quite a while
before that change gets out

00:29:49.270 --> 00:29:51.020
to all of your customers
and they actually

00:29:51.020 --> 00:29:52.700
see the new IP address
that they should

00:29:52.700 --> 00:29:55.250
be going to that's in Europe.

00:29:55.250 --> 00:29:57.100
But if we look at
the L7 balancer,

00:29:57.100 --> 00:29:59.080
it has automatically
failed all of our traffic

00:29:59.080 --> 00:30:00.730
over to the cluster
that is healthy,

00:30:00.730 --> 00:30:02.950
and all the responses now
are coming from Europe.

00:30:02.950 --> 00:30:03.760
And you can see
now, that they're

00:30:03.760 --> 00:30:05.740
bouncing back and forth
between the two different pods

00:30:05.740 --> 00:30:06.739
that we've scaled up to.

00:30:09.502 --> 00:30:11.710
And there's a little bit of
lag in the cloud console,

00:30:11.710 --> 00:30:14.030
but if we refresh
this in a second,

00:30:14.030 --> 00:30:16.510
we'll see that all of the
traffic is now overflowing,

00:30:16.510 --> 00:30:19.212
and it does mark here that all
of our backends are unhealthy.

00:30:22.194 --> 00:30:24.610
And with that, I think we're
to switch back to the slides.

00:30:29.774 --> 00:30:31.940
So the final area that I'm
going to talk about today

00:30:31.940 --> 00:30:33.050
is portability.

00:30:33.050 --> 00:30:34.550
As I mentioned
earlier, we recognize

00:30:34.550 --> 00:30:37.091
that not everyone has the luxury
of running their application

00:30:37.091 --> 00:30:38.690
in Google's Cloud.

00:30:38.690 --> 00:30:40.550
One of the primary
tenants of Kubernetes

00:30:40.550 --> 00:30:42.440
is that we should
have portability

00:30:42.440 --> 00:30:44.364
across deployment environments.

00:30:44.364 --> 00:30:46.280
Kubernetes should run
great in Google's Cloud,

00:30:46.280 --> 00:30:48.500
it should run great on
premise, it should run great

00:30:48.500 --> 00:30:50.708
on different operating
systems and different hardware

00:30:50.708 --> 00:30:51.600
architectures.

00:30:51.600 --> 00:30:53.200
Anywhere you can
run containers, you

00:30:53.200 --> 00:30:54.950
should be able to run
Kubernetes to manage

00:30:54.950 --> 00:30:56.360
those containers for you.

00:30:56.360 --> 00:30:58.610
Now, it's really easy for
someone to stand up on stage

00:30:58.610 --> 00:31:01.370
and tell you these
platitudes about how easy it

00:31:01.370 --> 00:31:03.627
should be to run
Kubernetes anywhere,

00:31:03.627 --> 00:31:05.210
but what we want to
do is sort of show

00:31:05.210 --> 00:31:07.610
you through examples
of customers.

00:31:07.610 --> 00:31:09.740
And so, Poki is a
company who decided

00:31:09.740 --> 00:31:11.900
to rebuild their monolithic
application using

00:31:11.900 --> 00:31:13.646
micro services and
containers, and they

00:31:13.646 --> 00:31:15.770
chose to use Kubernetes
for their container-centric

00:31:15.770 --> 00:31:18.399
infrastructure.

00:31:18.399 --> 00:31:20.690
They launched Kubernetes in
their existing environment.

00:31:20.690 --> 00:31:22.398
They ran it themselves
for a little while

00:31:22.398 --> 00:31:25.220
and then they decided
it's a lot of work

00:31:25.220 --> 00:31:26.480
to keep up with updates.

00:31:26.480 --> 00:31:28.750
It's a lot of work to
manage my own cluster.

00:31:28.750 --> 00:31:31.010
We're going to try out
Google Container Engine.

00:31:31.010 --> 00:31:33.380
And so they switched to
Google Container Engine.

00:31:33.380 --> 00:31:35.930
Now we have a happy
customer, but in the process

00:31:35.930 --> 00:31:38.930
of migrating into Google
Container Engine, because all

00:31:38.930 --> 00:31:41.750
of their applications were
written to run on Kubernetes,

00:31:41.750 --> 00:31:43.430
it gave them the
confidence that if they

00:31:43.430 --> 00:31:45.800
do want to run anywhere
else, as long as they

00:31:45.800 --> 00:31:49.340
can run Kubernetes, they are
free to go wherever they like.

00:31:49.340 --> 00:31:53.240
So today they're happy customer,
but if they get dissatisfied,

00:31:53.240 --> 00:31:56.397
then they are free to
run it on GCE itself,

00:31:56.397 --> 00:31:58.730
without using Google Container
engine or, on bare metal,

00:31:58.730 --> 00:32:01.406
or anywhere else they'd like.

00:32:01.406 --> 00:32:02.780
And the reason
that this works is

00:32:02.780 --> 00:32:05.750
because Kubernetes
provides really good

00:32:05.750 --> 00:32:08.379
extensibility points.

00:32:08.379 --> 00:32:09.920
Kubernetes can scale
down all the way

00:32:09.920 --> 00:32:12.360
to running on your laptop if
you want to run a development

00:32:12.360 --> 00:32:12.860
environment.

00:32:12.860 --> 00:32:14.990
Again, we would not recommend
that for production.

00:32:14.990 --> 00:32:17.270
But it's great for testing
out your applications

00:32:17.270 --> 00:32:18.960
before you push
them to production.

00:32:18.960 --> 00:32:20.620
That means you can
run your containers

00:32:20.620 --> 00:32:22.661
while you're on an airplane
without having access

00:32:22.661 --> 00:32:23.384
to the internet.

00:32:23.384 --> 00:32:24.800
You can also run
it on bare metal.

00:32:24.800 --> 00:32:27.110
You can run it in many
different cloud environments.

00:32:27.110 --> 00:32:30.525
And the API implementation
Kubernetes is completely open.

00:32:30.525 --> 00:32:32.150
The whole system is
modular and you can

00:32:32.150 --> 00:32:33.407
replace different components.

00:32:33.407 --> 00:32:35.240
And the way that we
support different clouds

00:32:35.240 --> 00:32:37.260
is that we do replace
different opponents.

00:32:37.260 --> 00:32:39.620
So earlier in the
presentation and in the demo,

00:32:39.620 --> 00:32:43.010
we showed how we can integrate
with Google's networking,

00:32:43.010 --> 00:32:47.249
Google's cloud load balancer,
Google's L7 Load Balancer.

00:32:47.249 --> 00:32:49.040
When you're integrating
with those systems,

00:32:49.040 --> 00:32:50.980
you're expressing the
intent of your application

00:32:50.980 --> 00:32:52.855
through Kubernetes
objects, through services,

00:32:52.855 --> 00:32:54.560
and through Ingress objects.

00:32:54.560 --> 00:32:57.290
And on GCE, on Google
Container Engine,

00:32:57.290 --> 00:33:00.800
we then instantiate those
objects through Google's APIs.

00:33:00.800 --> 00:33:02.300
But if you're running
elsewhere, you

00:33:02.300 --> 00:33:04.091
can run a different
Ingress controller that

00:33:04.091 --> 00:33:07.880
might instantiate your L7
load balancer using NGINX

00:33:07.880 --> 00:33:11.720
Or you might instantiate a
service controller just using

00:33:11.720 --> 00:33:13.160
IP tables.

00:33:13.160 --> 00:33:15.170
So the whole system is
modular, and replaceable,

00:33:15.170 --> 00:33:17.742
and can run anywhere.

00:33:17.742 --> 00:33:19.200
And the other nice
thing about this

00:33:19.200 --> 00:33:21.420
is it your applications
are not written

00:33:21.420 --> 00:33:23.640
to depend on any
particular infrastructure.

00:33:23.640 --> 00:33:26.190
Your applications depend
upon the Kubernetes APIs

00:33:26.190 --> 00:33:29.640
and Kubernetes mediates those
APIs with the environment

00:33:29.640 --> 00:33:30.780
that they're running in.

00:33:30.780 --> 00:33:32.363
So we talked about
the network models.

00:33:32.363 --> 00:33:34.500
Another good example
of this is volumes.

00:33:34.500 --> 00:33:36.540
So if you're running
on Google's cloud,

00:33:36.540 --> 00:33:38.670
you're most likely
using Google's PDs,

00:33:38.670 --> 00:33:40.530
Google's persistent
disks, which can be

00:33:40.530 --> 00:33:42.330
mounted into your containers.

00:33:42.330 --> 00:33:43.920
But if you're running
on bare metal,

00:33:43.920 --> 00:33:45.852
you might instead have
persistent volumes

00:33:45.852 --> 00:33:46.810
that are backed by NFS.

00:33:49.430 --> 00:33:52.430
And this allows you to easily
migrate your application

00:33:52.430 --> 00:33:55.630
from the legacy deployments
into containers,

00:33:55.630 --> 00:33:57.320
and lift and shift
onto the cloud

00:33:57.320 --> 00:33:59.620
when you're ready to migrate.

00:33:59.620 --> 00:34:01.700
It further means that
after that migration,

00:34:01.700 --> 00:34:04.610
as we saw with Poki, that you
don't feel stuck where you went

00:34:04.610 --> 00:34:06.740
to, that you still
have the flexibility

00:34:06.740 --> 00:34:10.152
to go wherever you think is the
best place for your company.

00:34:10.152 --> 00:34:12.110
So we like to say this
puts your apps on wheels

00:34:12.110 --> 00:34:14.659
and lets you roll it around,
wherever you'd like to go.

00:34:14.659 --> 00:34:16.739
And with that, I'm going
to hand it back to Chen.

00:34:16.739 --> 00:34:18.113
CHEN GOLDBERG:
Thank you, Robert.

00:34:20.510 --> 00:34:23.929
So we've seen that production
changes everything, right?

00:34:23.929 --> 00:34:26.150
And we talked through
some of customer example

00:34:26.150 --> 00:34:27.980
how we, in Google, can help.

00:34:27.980 --> 00:34:30.679
And I forgot to mention,
maybe it's a nice story

00:34:30.679 --> 00:34:32.940
to tell now about Niantic.

00:34:32.940 --> 00:34:35.239
They started on
Kubernetes 1.3 and they

00:34:35.239 --> 00:34:40.170
had a specific size of cluster,
and they need to scale it up.

00:34:40.170 --> 00:34:42.710
At the same time,
we were releasing

00:34:42.710 --> 00:34:45.080
a new version of Kubernetes
that was supporting

00:34:45.080 --> 00:34:48.389
2000 VMs per cluster.

00:34:48.389 --> 00:34:51.469
So without disturbing anything,
and for us doing all the work

00:34:51.469 --> 00:34:55.350
for that, we were able to scale
them out to a bigger cluster.

00:34:55.350 --> 00:34:59.360
So we first upgraded a cluster,
the control plane, and then

00:34:59.360 --> 00:35:03.410
they were able to grow
to the scale they needed.

00:35:03.410 --> 00:35:05.010
We saw auto scaling.

00:35:05.010 --> 00:35:08.060
We talked about access
control, and load balancing,

00:35:08.060 --> 00:35:10.520
and of course, portability.

00:35:10.520 --> 00:35:12.872
We want to believe
that you have a choice

00:35:12.872 --> 00:35:14.330
and we want to keep
you that choice

00:35:14.330 --> 00:35:16.640
and have the freedom
to make a decision.

00:35:16.640 --> 00:35:18.200
We commit to be the best, OK?

00:35:18.200 --> 00:35:20.012
And this is our
goal, to make sure

00:35:20.012 --> 00:35:21.470
that this is the
best place for you

00:35:21.470 --> 00:35:24.640
to run your containerized
applications.

00:35:24.640 --> 00:35:27.350
But all I speak about
is about today, right?

00:35:27.350 --> 00:35:29.030
And again, as I
said, when we are

00:35:29.030 --> 00:35:32.150
making a decision about where
to run our production workloads,

00:35:32.150 --> 00:35:35.060
we want to make sure
that we can be confident

00:35:35.060 --> 00:35:37.760
that this is the right platform
and the right decision for us

00:35:37.760 --> 00:35:39.850
for the future.

00:35:39.850 --> 00:35:41.950
I really like this
quote from Sam

00:35:41.950 --> 00:35:45.190
because it's able to
capture in one quote, both

00:35:45.190 --> 00:35:48.820
the today and the future.

00:35:48.820 --> 00:35:51.910
And the first thing he
mentions is about Kubernetes

00:35:51.910 --> 00:35:57.660
and why Kubernetes is production
ready today and the future.

00:35:57.660 --> 00:36:00.370
I think one thing to note
is about the community.

00:36:00.370 --> 00:36:03.340
The community has been
growing very fast.

00:36:03.340 --> 00:36:08.630
It's one of the top 10 projects
in Git-Hub from engagement,

00:36:08.630 --> 00:36:12.170
and looking on the
1500 contributors--

00:36:12.170 --> 00:36:16.280
I think more interesting even
to say that out of those 43,000

00:36:16.280 --> 00:36:19.190
commits, more than 50%
of them are actually

00:36:19.190 --> 00:36:20.760
not done by Googlers.

00:36:20.760 --> 00:36:23.960
This means that the pace of
innovation is mind blowing.

00:36:23.960 --> 00:36:26.240
OK, the new features,
the new requirements

00:36:26.240 --> 00:36:28.400
are really there to make
sure that everywhere you

00:36:28.400 --> 00:36:30.350
take Kubernetes and
run your containers,

00:36:30.350 --> 00:36:33.360
you will have a
production ready system.

00:36:33.360 --> 00:36:35.930
I also believe that this
really raises the bar.

00:36:35.930 --> 00:36:37.700
When we speak about
when to release

00:36:37.700 --> 00:36:41.150
a new version of Kubernetes,
we look at all environments,

00:36:41.150 --> 00:36:43.520
and we are making sure that
it works well everywhere,

00:36:43.520 --> 00:36:45.590
and we have users
and contributors

00:36:45.590 --> 00:36:47.900
that are doing the same.

00:36:47.900 --> 00:36:50.180
What we say, for example,
if you are planning to grow

00:36:50.180 --> 00:36:53.060
and you want to now have
a higher new professional

00:36:53.060 --> 00:36:55.250
people to manage your cluster.

00:36:55.250 --> 00:36:57.330
When you choose Google
Container Engine,

00:36:57.330 --> 00:36:59.390
it's the same as
any other Kubernetes

00:36:59.390 --> 00:37:01.910
deployment from that
knowledge perspective.

00:37:01.910 --> 00:37:05.210
So having 200 meet-ups
around the world

00:37:05.210 --> 00:37:07.820
allows you to hire those
professional to join the team,

00:37:07.820 --> 00:37:13.070
without the need to train them
specifically to your system.

00:37:13.070 --> 00:37:15.910
And the last thing is that
having that modularity,

00:37:15.910 --> 00:37:19.024
by having different projects
based on Kubernetes,

00:37:19.024 --> 00:37:20.690
you can change your
monitoring solution.

00:37:20.690 --> 00:37:22.640
We talked a lot about
the infrastructure,

00:37:22.640 --> 00:37:25.340
but also the tooling around it.

00:37:25.340 --> 00:37:27.140
Your DevOps tool
chain, you're logging,

00:37:27.140 --> 00:37:30.290
your monitoring,
tracing, all those things

00:37:30.290 --> 00:37:32.960
can be changed with new
things that will come along.

00:37:36.230 --> 00:37:38.800
So to summarize, Google
Container Engine benefits

00:37:38.800 --> 00:37:41.380
is first of all, pure
upstream Kubernetes.

00:37:41.380 --> 00:37:44.120
This is the fastest way
to get the Kubernetes

00:37:44.120 --> 00:37:47.050
releases available to
you in a reliable manner.

00:37:47.050 --> 00:37:48.820
We bring our 12
years of experience

00:37:48.820 --> 00:37:52.240
of managing production
systems on containers to you

00:37:52.240 --> 00:37:55.030
by managing and upgrading
your cluster, your control

00:37:55.030 --> 00:37:56.920
plan, and your VMs.

00:37:56.920 --> 00:37:59.290
And you get all the benefits
and all the goodness

00:37:59.290 --> 00:38:00.850
of the Google Cloud platform.

00:38:00.850 --> 00:38:04.950
You can really focus on
your application deployment.

00:38:04.950 --> 00:38:06.390
Thank you very much.

00:38:06.390 --> 00:38:09.740
[MUSIC PLAYING]

